00:03:35.461 --> 00:03:41.230
So what we plan to do here
is kind of share our journey

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.690
that led us to build an
application development

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:44.980
platform.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.570
So I'll share the landscape
we had prior to building

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:54.400
the platform at Target, the
problem we're trying to solve,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.860
and then we will
talk a bit about some

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.140
of the principles that
formed the basis of building

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:00.915
this application platform.

00:04:00.915 --> 00:04:02.290
And then we'll
share a little bit

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:05.500
about that inner
workings of the platform,

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:11.230
and we'll give you a walkthrough
of how a developer interacts

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:12.840
with the platform.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.890
And we'll finish
off sharing with you

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:19.579
folks what's the current status
of it and our future plans,

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:20.079
so to speak.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:25.738
So how we got here.

00:04:25.738 --> 00:04:27.780
I think many of you who
have been in the industry

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:32.520
for a while can
probably relate to this.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.530
How it goes about is
application development

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:38.580
teams need to build
and deploy like an app,

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:41.760
they reach out generally
to infrastructure teams,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.040
they make a specific request,
the infra teams place an order,

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.350
wait for the servers to arrive,
we rack them and turn it over

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:55.590
to our development teams,
and kind of use it.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.095
Simple, right?

00:04:57.095 --> 00:05:00.390
But it's kind of complex
at the same time.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:05.590
We even did service enable
the access to the computer.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.670
But it only provided,
again, incremental benefit.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:13.290
So what are the problems that we
faced just by providing access

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.110
to compute directly?

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.680
Infrastructure change fatigue.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.470
The teams who got
their pet servers

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:26.160
ended up spending a
lot of time patching.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.970
Infra teams like ours ended
up spending a lot of time

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:36.660
tacking abandoned servers on new
servers, doing server upgrades.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.550
For service enabled platforms
like OpenStack, Kubernetes,

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.700
we still ended up asking
teams to do the work,

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.850
to make changes to
support the platform,

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.600
to do upgrades, migrations,
things like that.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.110
So development team still end
up with a significant amount

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:59.840
of infrastructure work in
their backlog, basically.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.860
And this has been kind
of disruptive for them.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.340
So that's one part.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.250
Now, there is the other part
from a developer standpoint.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:16.430
So the developer faced
very disparate experiences.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:20.210
They need to deploy applications
to our data centers,

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.310
to our stores, our distribution
centers, to Google Cloud.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.420
Deployment to
OpenStack in our data

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:32.000
centers very different than how
do we deploy to Google Cloud

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.811
or to our edge locations
that run Kubernetes?

00:06:35.811 --> 00:06:38.480
These are all different.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.540
So the development team ended
up using different deployment

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:46.590
tools, and they
had to build them,

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:50.500
they had to maintain
these tool chains,

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:52.530
and this is work for
the development teams.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:57.040
Application
development teams like

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.350
that support our distributed--

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:02.410
that work with our distributed
platforms like stores

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:06.580
sometimes end up spending even
70% of their development time

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:11.140
in deploying workloads to these
distributed edge locations.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.150
Plus on top of this,
there is no standards

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:20.290
in terms of how teams can
implement logging, monitoring,

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:22.468
basic operational stuff.

00:07:22.468 --> 00:07:24.010
Sometimes there are
too many options,

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:25.882
sometimes there are
too less options,

00:07:25.882 --> 00:07:27.340
so it's kind of
hard for developers

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:30.190
to figure out what's the
right pattern to use.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:36.990
So we started off by
removing technical debt.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:40.570
So what's technical debt?

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:42.750
We declare it as anything.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:46.500
It can be obsolete
hardware, underused

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:49.410
applications, anything
that prevents you

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:54.130
basically from building
capacity in a modern technology.

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:57.600
So we started working towards
removing technical debt.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.500
So we literally like
removed physical hardware

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:03.930
from our data centers,
which are legacy.

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.060
We removed thousands
of equipments

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.080
from our stores,
old PDAs, servers,

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.350
and just we retired
hundreds of applications.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:19.740
So just to help teams get
rid of these legacy backlogs

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:23.110
so that they can
focus on building new.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:24.630
And how does it help?

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.420
So one, it helps
by us not having

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:31.080
to pay millions of dollars in
terms of vendor licensing cost.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.049
And this also frees up
application development teams

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:37.380
to start building more
than applications.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.039
And we started with that, and
we gave them direct access

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:45.030
to our service enabled
compute platforms,

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:47.400
and that doesn't help.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.440
They will basically
end up with a whole lot

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:55.240
more work when the lifecycle of
these compute services come up.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:59.160
And this is where
TAP comes into play,

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.610
so Jim will talk about how
we approached that problem.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:03.452
JIM BEYERS: Thanks, Jay.

00:09:03.452 --> 00:09:06.620
So Jay outlined the
history as to why we

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:08.450
decided we needed to build TAP.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:10.400
I think you probably
all can relate

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:14.210
pretty closely with a lot of
things he was talking about.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.930
So before we decided how to
do what we're going to do,

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:21.530
we decided to sort of the
why, if that makes sense.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.630
And honestly, even before
we started with the why

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:24.830
we started with the who.

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:28.790
So a peer of Jay and ours, a
guy by the name of Dan Woods,

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:30.380
came up with this
concept of someone

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:33.100
that he referred to as
the midnight developer.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:34.670
And the midnight
developer is someone

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:37.850
who is starting from nothing,
she may have never interacted

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:40.690
with the platform before,
maybe she started today,

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:42.440
and she's trying to
get something together

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.810
for her demo the next day.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.090
If she can't build a
fully featured demo

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:50.270
without having to have a
meeting, or a Service Now

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:53.120
request, or whatever your
ticketing platform is,

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:56.140
we've failed as a team.

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:58.160
And disclaimer, don't
work at midnight.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:00.230
Have a life.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:03.798
So the six principles that we
came up with are as follows.

00:10:03.798 --> 00:10:05.840
So the first one's self
service like I mentioned,

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:07.532
it's zero [INAUDIBLE]
up and running.

00:10:07.532 --> 00:10:09.740
Empower the teams to get
their project off the ground

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:12.440
without needing to interface
with any of the platform teams.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:14.540
No meetings, basically.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.207
Common patterns of usability.

00:10:16.207 --> 00:10:18.290
So teams will interact
with the platform offerings

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:19.070
in a common way.

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:22.220
So when Jay was talking
about disparate experiences,

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.680
if you have two write Chef
scripts to deploy things

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:28.100
to stores and you are using
Spinnaker deployed to GCP,

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:30.080
that's--

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:32.780
we're not having a
common experience.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:35.480
Discoverability, teams should
be able to quickly and easily

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.460
ascertain what the capabilities
are that are available to them

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:38.127
on the platform.

00:10:38.127 --> 00:10:40.740
So essentially similar
to the no meetings,

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.010
it should be like
not no docs, but it

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:46.740
should be fairly intuitive
to open up the page,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.920
look at what you need to
do, I need a load balancer,

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:49.700
click a button.

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:52.880
Not read for 30 minutes or try
and find some tribal knowledge.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:55.460
No searching wiki pages.

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:58.340
Availability, it's got
to be up all the time.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:03.453
We're not going have maintenance
windows, and dev is prod.

00:11:03.453 --> 00:11:04.620
Everything's an application.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.280
TAP uses TAP, we
bootstrap TAP with itself.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:09.870
That's a big broad conversation.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:11.370
If you'd like to
hear more about it,

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:13.417
I'd have to talk to
you about it after.

00:11:13.417 --> 00:11:15.000
And then strong
opinions loosely held.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.110
So that means that the
decisions that we made today

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.670
for the underlying
implementations

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:22.470
of the componentry
of the platform

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:25.980
may not be the appropriate
decisions for the underlying

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.700
componentry of the
platform tomorrow.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:29.640
So we need to build the
platform in such a way

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.980
that we can swap things in
and out interchangeably,

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:35.580
and we've done that quite
a bit in the last year.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:36.932
So what do we build?

00:11:36.932 --> 00:11:38.390
We built TAP, we
already know that.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:41.420
It's the Target
Application Platform.

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:46.310
And TAP is this
consistent experience

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:48.050
like we outlined
in the principles.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:50.450
What tap is not is
GitHub Enterprise.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:52.700
It is not Artifact Read,
it is not Spinnaker,

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:54.857
it is not Kubernetes,
it is not OpenStack,

00:11:54.857 --> 00:11:55.940
it's none of these things.

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:58.100
It's essentially the
overlying platform

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:01.010
that abstracts you away
from these independent tools

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:04.430
so that you've got a consistent
experience across all of them.

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:06.950
It's like the glue code
that we wrote to do this.

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:11.190
And we did this by
starting by saying,

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:13.010
like, we're going
to look at what's

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:18.020
the fundamental underlying
runtime components that we

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.210
need to have, and obviously
one of those is compute.

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:22.820
So Kubernetes is a fantastic
container scheduler.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:26.960
It's also a great platform,
but it's a fantastic container

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:28.070
scheduler.

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:30.770
So we started with the
idea of let's figure

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:33.080
out how we can
leverage the scheduling

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:35.720
aspects of Kubernetes,
but open up

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:40.233
so that we can use other pieces
of the runtime components

00:12:40.233 --> 00:12:41.150
other than Kubernetes.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:42.692
So in other words,
what I'm saying is

00:12:42.692 --> 00:12:45.350
we're not just dumping
everything into Kubernetes.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:48.560
So what we did was
we wrote shim layers

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:51.680
in front of each
deployment of Kubernetes

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:53.390
so that we're deploying
through the shim.

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:54.807
So what that means
specifically is

00:12:54.807 --> 00:12:56.660
we decided to use the
Kubernetes deployment

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.610
spec for every
deployment when we

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:01.130
deploy to a compute provider.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:07.190
And what that allows us to do
is to have multiple instances

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:10.315
of Kubernetes that could be
potentially different versions,

00:13:10.315 --> 00:13:12.440
they can be running on
obviously different compute,

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:16.550
and ultimately that's all
abstracted away from the users.

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:20.750
We recently ran into the
Kubernetes 1.6 vulnerability

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:22.280
I'm sure everybody is used to.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:24.572
There were break-in changes
in the contract between 1.6

00:13:24.572 --> 00:13:27.003
and whatever we went to, 1.14,
that was work for people.

00:13:27.003 --> 00:13:29.420
And that goes back to what Jay
was saying before about how

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:30.878
we don't want them
to have to worry

00:13:30.878 --> 00:13:32.180
about these kinds of things.

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:34.760
The other really cool thing
that this allows us to do

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:38.030
is to present other
compute providers

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:39.840
with the same deployment spec.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:42.170
So you write your
app once, and you

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:44.540
can deploy it to Kubernetes,
you can deploy it--

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:48.200
we started with OpenStack
for our VM provider which

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:50.480
solved a different problem.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:55.040
Whereas before we ran into some
issues with large installations

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:57.500
of compute services
providers like OpenStack

00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:00.890
with doing things like
infrastructure upgrades,

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:03.680
now we can do our favorite
tagline, more of them

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:06.860
smaller so that if
we want to take out

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:08.990
some compute provider for
maintenance or something

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.510
like that, all we have to
do is have teams redeploy.

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:13.820
We can take things offline,
nobody even needs to know,

00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:15.710
which is pretty sweet.

00:14:15.710 --> 00:14:19.220
And so we can do the same thing
with other compute providers,

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:21.340
rinse repeat.

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:24.190
How do we deploy to
those compute providers?

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:29.050
We wrote what we call this
platform intelligent engine,

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:34.390
or PIE, that implements the
Kubernetes deployment spec also

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:36.700
and then makes an intelligent
placement decision to one

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:38.050
of those compute providers.

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:40.432
Now to be honest, the
actual intelligence

00:14:40.432 --> 00:14:41.890
of the platform
intelligence engine

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:44.800
right now is not
particularly intelligent.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:47.597
We taken some of
the different specs,

00:14:47.597 --> 00:14:49.180
like we have these
other concepts I'll

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:50.722
touch on a little
bit like pool type,

00:14:50.722 --> 00:14:52.780
or if you're a
compliant workload,

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.690
or if you want to be a VM
or a container or runtime,

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:57.210
and then we make a decision.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:59.460
And some of the ways that
we do that right now is just

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:02.290
by waiting or giving preference
to specific what we call pie

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.690
slices-- that's a
compute provider--

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:06.420
to say, hey, deploy here.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:09.460
A really crazy and great story
is Jay's team wrote something

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:11.500
that we call SGDM.

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:13.570
It stands for Server
Group Deployment Manager,

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:16.540
and all it does is
it's a VM runtime.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:19.960
Dead simple, runs VMs
really, really well.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:25.840
And we moved almost all of
our non-compliant workloads

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:28.000
that we're running on
OpenStack over to SGDM,

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.680
and none of our users even
knew, which is pretty rad.

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:34.540
So that's that.

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:37.392
So that covers the
compute provider aspects.

00:15:37.392 --> 00:15:39.100
The other areas
obviously of the platform

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:42.010
is this is not just
running CPU cycles.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.770
We've got things like config,
and secrets, load balancing,

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:48.490
log metrics, and potentially
things like an API layer or API

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:49.750
proxy.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:53.560
Today, I should say we started
with console for config.

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:56.260
We've taken out
pieces of console

00:15:56.260 --> 00:15:58.420
over the course of our
journey and replaced them

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:01.780
with some homegrown
pieces, et cetera.

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:04.560
And our users didn't
know, which was great.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:08.010
We used Vault for secrets, we're
using Fabio for load balancing.

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:09.760
When you deploy for
instance, basically it

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:11.343
auto registers with
the load balancer,

00:16:11.343 --> 00:16:12.850
which is pretty sweet.

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:16.095
And this goes back to
that principle of the fact

00:16:16.095 --> 00:16:18.220
that we might make decisions
today that tomorrow we

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:20.600
might want to change.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.990
So I'm going to
do a walkthrough,

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:25.720
but I want to cover
some basic core concepts

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:28.360
and terminology that's going to
be somewhat probably important.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:29.777
I'll try and go
over this quickly,

00:16:29.777 --> 00:16:34.270
because maybe it's going to
be rudimentary for people.

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:36.190
So an application is
essentially a project.

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:39.280
You start your application
inside of the UI

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:40.660
or through the API.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:42.755
Everything in TAP
is API first, so you

00:16:42.755 --> 00:16:45.130
don't have to interact with
the API if you don't want to.

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:48.460
You can use a CLI that we
also have, it's pretty great.

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:52.870
Application is essentially
the container for your code.

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:55.277
Inside of an application,
there's a concept of a cluster.

00:16:55.277 --> 00:16:56.860
An application can
have many clusters.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:59.050
Clusters belong
to an environment.

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:02.080
We only have two,
there's dev and prod.

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:04.839
Clusters are the logical
organization of infrastructure.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:07.000
You can have one or many
deployments, like I said.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.880
So you deploy to a cluster.

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:11.359
Instances and server groups.

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:14.450
So you're docker container,
or your VM is an instance.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.560
You can have one to many of
them inside of a server group.

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:20.327
That's your basically
unit of deployment.

00:17:20.327 --> 00:17:22.119
One of the things that
we do require inside

00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:23.911
of every application
is that every instance

00:17:23.911 --> 00:17:26.660
has an instance health check.

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:28.780
So you can run any
docker container

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:31.450
you want on the platform, but
it has to have a health check.

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:33.130
And the reason that
we implemented that

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:36.070
is so that if your application
is up and healthy as determined

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:39.010
by you, then it will
register with a load balancer

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:40.660
and start taking traffic.

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:42.500
Currently we only
take HDTV traffic,

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:45.160
but that's in flight
very quick for us

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:48.053
to be able to do TCPL also.

00:17:48.053 --> 00:17:49.720
And then there's this
concept of stacks.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.400
So as I said
before, applications

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:53.400
are globally namespaced.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:55.150
Stacks are also
globally namespaced such

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:57.160
that you can have a--

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:59.530
so Red Sky's an
application at Target.

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:04.948
You can have a Red Sky UAT, you
can have a Red Sky performance,

00:18:04.948 --> 00:18:06.490
you can have a Red
Sky red sky if you

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:08.050
want to all in production.

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:11.470
And so that allows you to
wire different deployments

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:14.380
of your application together so
that you don't get interference

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:18.100
if that's what you want, say,
specifically for performance.

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:20.122
So that covers the sort
of high level concepts.

00:18:20.122 --> 00:18:21.580
I just wanted to
say because you're

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:23.650
in to see some of
those in the UIs.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:26.590
We weren't brave enough
to actually do a demo,

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:32.680
but I have video shots, video
screenshots of the app here.

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:34.930
And I'd be happy to show
anybody this later if they're

00:18:34.930 --> 00:18:36.305
interested, and
I can get on VPN.

00:18:39.057 --> 00:18:41.390
All, right bear with me because
taking video screenshots

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:42.682
of stuff is a little bit wonky.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:49.550
So-- shoot, see I
already went too fast.

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:51.940
So this shows here that
basically my screen

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:53.860
as a user of TAP.

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:55.810
Shows all the teams that
I'm on inside of TAP

00:18:55.810 --> 00:18:57.370
because you might want
to be able to allow

00:18:57.370 --> 00:18:59.560
other people to deploy your
application besides just

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:00.190
yourself.

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:01.288
Seems reasonable, right?

00:19:01.288 --> 00:19:03.580
I'm going to walk you through
what we call quick start.

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:05.823
I'm going to create
an app on TAP.

00:19:05.823 --> 00:19:07.990
So I'm going to click that
button, I'm going to say,

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:09.610
I want to start a
new project, or I

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:12.120
could start with existing code.

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:14.370
If you ever used a platform
as a service, some of this

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:15.660
should look familiar to you.

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:19.050
But obviously, some of
it's Target specific.

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:20.970
So I'm going to choose
my application name.

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:23.700
It's my next demo, I think.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:24.370
Yep.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:27.150
And I'm just going to make sure
that it's available if it is.

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:29.790
Now this is mine.

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:32.070
I'll come back to this, but
essentially when it gets

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:36.810
deployed it's
mynextdemo.dev.target.com.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:41.760
Or if I'm in prod it's
mynextdemo.prod.target.com.

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:45.330
Hit that, you go directly
to the app or the stack

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:46.650
if you're using stacks.

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:49.410
Blossom ID is essentially
just like a configuration ID.

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:51.300
It's like a metadata
service at Target

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:54.330
that allows us to do
things like charge backs--

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.040
or not charge backs,
sorry-- show back,

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:58.800
so that we can show how much
specific apps are costing,

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:00.360
et cetera, or to
be able to tie back

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:03.127
to a particular
line of business.

00:20:03.127 --> 00:20:04.710
And then the team,
like I talked about

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:06.960
before, if I want to give
access to other people

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:08.550
to manage my application
inside of TAP

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:10.280
I can add them to my team.

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:12.480
And I'm going to add
my contacting email

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.220
in my description,
and then I'm going

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:16.650
to say, hey, what GitHub
organization do I want?

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:19.820
And I'm going to choose me,
and I'm going to say, go.

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:21.083
And then I'm going to decide--

00:20:21.083 --> 00:20:23.000
here's the four things
that we support so far.

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:28.250
So Praxis is an internal target
UI react set of components.

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:31.770
It's more or less how most front
end stuff is written in Target.

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:35.510
So I can click Praxis app,
I can do a Spring Boot app,

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:38.030
I can do a Ratpack app
or a Ratpack with Groovy.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:40.940
But 85% of the target apps
are Spring Boot, we think,

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:42.500
so I'm just going to do that.

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:44.460
Also, it's the one
I know how to do.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:46.563
And then I get to decide
between a container

00:20:46.563 --> 00:20:47.480
and a virtual machine.

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:50.570
So like we said before,
it's not specific

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:52.323
to the actual compute
provider because we

00:20:52.323 --> 00:20:54.740
want to have the capability
of being able to decide what's

00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:55.970
the best thing for today.

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:59.000
So maybe today it's
OpenStack-- but it's not--

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.680
now it's SGDM.

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:02.480
So we decided to
do virtual machine

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:04.580
so that we could make
those decisions as we're

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:06.580
the ones that are supporting
the infrastructure.

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:09.530
And the same thing we believe is
true for containers-- probably

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:11.000
Kubernetes, though.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:13.368
So I chose container,
and it's just

00:21:13.368 --> 00:21:14.910
telling me what it's
going to do now,

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:17.035
which is going to scaffold
out some code in GitHub,

00:21:17.035 --> 00:21:19.503
it's going to create
my Drone config,

00:21:19.503 --> 00:21:21.170
and it's going to run
my build in Drone.

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:24.380
So drones are a CI tool if you
guys aren't familiar with it,

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:30.120
and then it's going to create
my cluster and deploy my code.

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:32.390
So I forgot that I had
to be signed into Drone

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:35.370
when I made this.

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:37.500
So I signed into Drone
and then it did it.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:39.860
So you can see here that
it actually had already

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:43.858
been working but I wasn't signed
in, and so it built my code.

00:21:43.858 --> 00:21:45.650
So like I said, this
is a little bit wonky.

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:47.230
Give me a second.

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:49.170
There we go, sorry.

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:51.290
So after that, this
is just a short screen

00:21:51.290 --> 00:21:53.270
that shows essentially
that we met

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:56.960
the scaffolded code in GitHub.

00:21:56.960 --> 00:22:02.160
It ran my Drone job, and
it created the cluster.

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:09.950
And then after that, it
actually created the cluster

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:12.020
but I wanted to show
what it's like for you

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:12.900
to create your own.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:15.890
So this concept is
the concept of stacks.

00:22:15.890 --> 00:22:17.528
So this goes back
into like if I wanted

00:22:17.528 --> 00:22:19.820
to have different environments
inside of my environment

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:22.070
because I wanted to test
out different lines of code,

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:23.900
here's my next demo dash dev.

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:24.590
It's available.

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:26.382
I'm going to click
Continue, and then these

00:22:26.382 --> 00:22:27.773
are just some of
the config items

00:22:27.773 --> 00:22:28.940
that people can select from.

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:33.020
So today we only allow to
deploy to the data center.

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:35.300
We started some work on
being able to deploy it

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:36.765
to public cloud or to GCP.

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:42.710
And then also obviously
as Jay mentioned,

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:45.440
our goal is to be able
to deploy workloads also

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:50.810
to stores and to
distribution centers.

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:53.770
So pool type is
something that we also

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:55.520
allow for you to be
able to sort of define

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:57.812
if you've got constraints
with respect to your runtime.

00:22:57.812 --> 00:22:59.840
So if you are a Sox
application, you

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:03.020
would choose that you
wanted the Sox pool type.

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:04.760
We have some special
sort of stuff

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:07.070
that we reserve only to
certain users at Target.

00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:09.650
So like there's a group
that's doing work on GPU.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:13.840
We created a PIE slice that was
able to deploy to some GPUs,

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:16.340
and they're able to see that
in here, which is pretty slick.

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:18.757
And then there's the proprietor
type like I chose earlier,

00:23:18.757 --> 00:23:22.170
there is a container
in the M runtime.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:23.730
And then the region
at the end is

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:29.010
we've got TTC and TTE are
the two target data centers,

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:32.160
and then there's also
an option to choose HA.

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:35.400
I'll come back to that
because this is essentially

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:37.850
just like my app is
running on port 8080, which

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:39.993
is the one that Load
Balancer is going to hit.

00:23:39.993 --> 00:23:42.660
And then I got some choices that
look very similar to Kubernetes

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.118
with respect to
how many millicores

00:23:44.118 --> 00:23:46.033
and how much memory I want.

00:23:46.033 --> 00:23:47.700
This is the health
check I talked about.

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:50.310
So you can implement
your health check however

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:51.780
you want, but you
have to have one

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:54.420
so that we know that your app
is up and available because we

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:56.310
don't want the load
balancer to route traffic

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:59.708
to garbage instances
that are not working.

00:23:59.708 --> 00:24:01.500
This is just basically
the config for that.

00:24:04.360 --> 00:24:06.260
This is the deployment strategy.

00:24:06.260 --> 00:24:09.510
Today we only support two,
red-black, or blue-green

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:14.060
depending on if you are
more akin to Netflix,

00:24:14.060 --> 00:24:15.470
and then Highlander.

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:18.830
So deploy my instance group,
wait for it to be healthy,

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:20.870
and then I'll start
running traffic to it

00:24:20.870 --> 00:24:24.205
or deploy it and then destroy
the other one right away.

00:24:24.205 --> 00:24:25.830
Which I'm embarrassed
to say it took me

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:27.720
a long time to realize why
it was called Highlander.

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:28.637
There can be only one.

00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:33.150
So a server group
capacity is just how many

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:34.560
instances do I want to deploy?

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:35.470
You can say one.

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:36.720
I don't think we have a limit.

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:40.590
I know for sure that there's
some people that have 80.

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:43.777
Actually, it may
be more than that.

00:24:43.777 --> 00:24:46.110
Choose the size of the number
of instances that you want

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:46.800
and then deploy.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:50.130
We do have plans in the
future to be able to do things

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:52.200
with the platform
intelligence engine to say,

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:55.290
like, hey, someone today
might say, hey man,

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:56.670
I'm going to get a million TPS.

00:24:56.670 --> 00:24:58.210
Well, that's
generally never true.

00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:00.390
And so they might
weigh over provision,

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:02.580
and we could have
the potential--

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:06.750
since every app
that's deployed is

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:11.190
using the runtime connector
is automatically instrumented

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:12.420
with logs of metrics.

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:14.070
So we know the
health of everything

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:16.278
as long as it's running with
a health check and point

00:25:16.278 --> 00:25:19.568
and it's using the
runtime connector.

00:25:19.568 --> 00:25:21.610
So we would be able to do
what I'm talking about,

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:27.040
which is if it's taking two
TPS and running at 0.01%

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:29.777
CPU utilization, we probably
would scale them down

00:25:29.777 --> 00:25:30.360
in the future.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:36.190
And then like I said before,
everything is API first.

00:25:36.190 --> 00:25:38.540
So you'll see here
that that's essentially

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:41.300
the JSON of what the
API will look like.

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:46.180
Next, details.

00:25:49.370 --> 00:25:53.745
So now that I've deployed my
cluster, it's up and running.

00:25:53.745 --> 00:25:55.870
I'm just going to go and
check and say, hey, here's

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:57.580
my pipeline.

00:25:57.580 --> 00:26:01.060
We're using pieces of
Spinnaker under the covers

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:03.920
to run deployments for TAP.

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:05.590
There's quite a bit
of custom pieces

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:07.423
that we've taken out
and rewritten ourselves

00:26:07.423 --> 00:26:10.930
because they were more akin
to our use case, I'll say.

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:13.960
So essentially, that's
your Spinnaker pipeline--

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:15.850
create the server group,
disable the old one,

00:26:15.850 --> 00:26:17.428
and create the new one.

00:26:17.428 --> 00:26:18.970
Here's the UI for
config and secrets.

00:26:18.970 --> 00:26:23.195
So for console for config
and alt for secrets,

00:26:23.195 --> 00:26:24.820
you can enter your
stuff in right here,

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:26.980
and it will get injected
in your application

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:30.640
provided you've written
your code correctly.

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:33.023
And this is basically
going back and being

00:26:33.023 --> 00:26:35.440
able to edit those constraints
I showed you earlier if you

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:36.645
want to do your deployment.

00:26:36.645 --> 00:26:38.770
It's not an uncommon thing
for us to find customers

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:40.330
that deploy to the
container runtime

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:42.843
and find out that they're
actually very compute-intensive

00:26:42.843 --> 00:26:45.010
workloads that they decide
that it's better for them

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:46.400
to run on VMs.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:49.940
That's actually quite common.

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:56.740
And oh yeah, so inside
my group of server groups

00:26:56.740 --> 00:26:58.900
there would be if I had
more than one instance, all

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:00.130
the individual instance.

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:02.620
I can go directly
to my instance logs,

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:04.240
directly to my health
check in points.

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:05.698
So I clicked on
that, that takes me

00:27:05.698 --> 00:27:07.480
to the logs specific
for that runtime--

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:08.816
sorry, for that container.

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:17.320
There is health checks
specifically for that instance.

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:24.920
And so this is pretty slick.

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:27.700
And the reason I said
before about the HA,

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:29.840
I was happy to have
caught one earlier.

00:27:29.840 --> 00:27:33.290
But basically,
what we see here is

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:39.880
a sort of production view of the
Platform Intelligence Engine.

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:44.380
So on the bottom are a list
of all of the PIE slices,

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:46.300
which if you remember
is the facade that sits

00:27:46.300 --> 00:27:48.400
in front of a compute provider.

00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:50.590
So you can kind of infer
what most of them are.

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:53.230
The first one is
K8's provider that's

00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:56.560
running inside of
our TTS data center,

00:27:56.560 --> 00:27:59.288
and we've got 1,300
instances in there.

00:27:59.288 --> 00:28:01.330
You can see that overall,
of all of TAP we've got

00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:05.620
a little over 20,000 running.

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:09.633
And I think it comes
right at 20 seconds.

00:28:09.633 --> 00:28:11.550
You can actually see in
the PIE slice activity

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:16.093
the little spiral dealeo actual
deployments that are going on,

00:28:16.093 --> 00:28:17.010
which is kind of cool.

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:20.040
And right at 20 seconds,
you'll see that two happen,

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:21.180
and it's an HA deploy.

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:24.210
One, two, TTC and the
other one did TTCE.

00:28:27.328 --> 00:28:29.870
If you scroll down, then you'll
see some of those constraints

00:28:29.870 --> 00:28:31.725
like we talked about.

00:28:31.725 --> 00:28:33.350
Just about everything
in here, I think,

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:34.600
is-- well no, that's not true.

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:37.910
Most everything is obviously
gen pop, hence Gen population,

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:44.230
but you've got the Sox pool
type running on OpenStack.

00:28:44.230 --> 00:28:45.760
You've got Gen Pop
running on a VM,

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:48.240
this is that SGDM I
was talking about,

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:53.260
and there's a bunch of different
PIE slices in here, including I

00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:54.011
think the--

00:28:56.900 --> 00:28:57.672
I can't find it.

00:28:57.672 --> 00:28:58.380
I don't remember.

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:00.470
I know the GPU one is in here.

00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:04.480
There's like four GPUs,
it's not that cool.

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:08.740
So that is PIE, and
Jay's going to talk now

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:14.290
a little bit about the status
of TAP and where we're at today.

00:29:14.290 --> 00:29:15.760
JAY CHANDRASHEKARAN:
Thanks, Jim.

00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:17.650
Can we switch over
to the slides?

00:29:20.253 --> 00:29:21.170
JIM BEYERS: Oh, just--

00:29:21.170 --> 00:29:23.120
yeah, that's fine.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:27.290
JAY CHANDRASHEKARAN:
So current status.

00:29:27.290 --> 00:29:32.825
This was launched last year,
specifically like April 1st.

00:29:32.825 --> 00:29:35.580
It's not a talk as such.

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:39.290
So we're exactly one year
into building the platform

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:41.630
and running it.

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:44.150
And you can see
the adoption curve,

00:29:44.150 --> 00:29:47.150
it's just kind of rocketed up.

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:48.520
And this is real.

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:50.930
It's running production
workloads in Target,

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:53.720
supporting our business.

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:58.100
This chart is a few weeks
old, and the current one,

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:01.520
it's still growth is
like the same pace.

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:06.170
We now have about 5,400
applications running.

00:30:06.170 --> 00:30:12.880
We have about 20,000 instances
for deployments per minute.

00:30:12.880 --> 00:30:13.600
That's happening.

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:15.580
So we think about
it, the friction

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:19.300
that we are removing from
our development teams,

00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:25.960
they are doing like about
5,700 deployments per day.

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:28.510
And just by clicking
buttons, this

00:30:28.510 --> 00:30:31.570
is like the work they
don't have to do.

00:30:31.570 --> 00:30:33.910
So that's actual
advantage for us.

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:39.850
So future plans.

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:42.390
So our vision is
that TAP will be

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:46.080
one stop shop for
all compute and data

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:48.870
needs for our developers.

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:51.100
So we will accomplish
this by abstracting

00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:53.140
the compute and the
data provider layers

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:56.710
away from the developers
as Jim demoed it.

00:30:56.710 --> 00:30:59.410
So they no longer have to
worry about which version

00:30:59.410 --> 00:31:00.910
of Kubernetes they
are running on,

00:31:00.910 --> 00:31:03.910
whether they are
running on Google Cloud

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:04.985
or in our data centers.

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:11.980
Jim also demoed and
talked about PIE earlier.

00:31:11.980 --> 00:31:17.620
This component of the TAP will
become intelligent over time.

00:31:17.620 --> 00:31:20.320
And with that
intelligence we will

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:25.780
be able to make automatic
workload placements using TAP,

00:31:25.780 --> 00:31:27.580
basically determining
which is the best

00:31:27.580 --> 00:31:31.730
location to run the workload.

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:36.460
We also have a greater
ability to improve efficiency

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:38.830
of our compute platforms.

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:43.580
We can actually understand
applications of source usage,

00:31:43.580 --> 00:31:46.190
and even if they are
allocated a higher resource

00:31:46.190 --> 00:31:49.400
than what they actually
need the platform

00:31:49.400 --> 00:31:53.420
can actually dial it down.

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:56.360
We also have, of course, in
our custom compute platform

00:31:56.360 --> 00:32:00.830
we have much better
ability to overprovision--

00:32:00.830 --> 00:32:09.080
we actually see about 70% to 80%
of physical compute utilization

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:14.990
in our custom compute platform,
which is kind of great.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:18.515
So our plans.

00:32:18.515 --> 00:32:20.840
Jim kind of touched on it too.

00:32:20.840 --> 00:32:23.120
Currently our
focus is on getting

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:24.590
things running in data center.

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:28.010
We are going to switch over
to get this thing working

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:31.730
in Google Cloud and to our
edge locations and stores

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:34.070
and distribution centers.

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:35.630
We already have
Kubernetes clusters

00:32:35.630 --> 00:32:38.510
running in our stores
and distribution centers.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:42.050
The plan is to
have this platform

00:32:42.050 --> 00:32:45.170
that we build called
unimetrics, integrate that

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:50.150
with TAP so that developers
interact with TAP.

00:32:50.150 --> 00:32:54.600
And that's the way they will
deploy workloads in Target

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:57.320
they're not going to have any
of those disparate experiences.

00:33:01.020 --> 00:33:03.540
As I mentioned
earlier, we can move

00:33:03.540 --> 00:33:06.870
workloads that around based on
attributes like availability,

00:33:06.870 --> 00:33:09.480
response time.

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:14.610
With TAP, we should be able to
bus workload into Google Cloud

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:18.720
if we need to, or we can
automatically move workloads

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:24.450
to our edge if we need a better
response time or availability.

00:33:24.450 --> 00:33:28.170
So basically, we see
TAP as the one stop shop

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:33.070
for our developers to
interact with infrastructure.

00:33:33.070 --> 00:33:36.060
So we are excited
about our journey,

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:40.410
and we believe TAP gives
our developers the ability

00:33:40.410 --> 00:33:43.860
to focus on
delivering new feature

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:47.010
functions for our guests
and build productivity

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:49.500
tools for our team members.

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:53.430
And thanks to TAP, I believe
our application developers

00:33:53.430 --> 00:33:56.610
will be able to do this at
a much faster rate than any

00:33:56.610 --> 00:33:59.280
of our competitors
in our industry.

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:03.700
Thanks.

00:34:03.700 --> 00:34:04.950
JIM BEYERS: Thanks, everybody.

00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:08.000
[APPLAUSE]

